WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 84: The Treason Trial of Aaron Burr: The Duel, the Empire, and the Chief Justice

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.120
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:15.200
[Rebecca] In the summer of 187, the eyes of the young American Republic were riveted on the packed hall of the House of Delegates in Richmond, Virginia.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:29.360
[Rebecca] Sitting in the defendant's dock was Aaron Burr, revolutionary war hero, brilliant legal mind, former vice president of the United States, and the man who had shot Alexander Hamilton dead in an infamous duel three years earlier.

5
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:43.600
[Rebecca] Accused by President Thomas Jefferson of organizing an armed conspiracy to conquer Spanish Mexico, seize the Louisiana territory, and tear the Western states from the Union, Burr faced the death penalty by hanging for high treason.

6
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:49.760
[Rebecca] Presiding over the court was Chief Justice John Marshall, Jefferson's bitter ideological rival.

7
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:56.880
[Rebecca] George brings us the dramatic story of the treason trial of Aaron Burr.

8
00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:05.920
[George] In the founding era of the United States, few men possessed greater brilliance, aristocratic lineage, or volatile ambition than Aaron Burr.

9
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:12.160
[George] Born into intellectual royalty as the grandson of Jonathan Edwards and the son of Princeton's president.

10
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:40.240
[George] In the landmark election of 1800, Burr tied Thomas Jefferson in the Electoral College, and while Burr dutifully accepted the vice presidency, Jefferson never forgave him for allowing 36 ballots in the House of Representatives before yielding.

11
00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:53.280
[George] Ostracized by Jefferson's Democratic Republicans and thwarted in New York politics by Hamilton, Burr challenged Hamilton to their fatal duel on the rocky bluffs of Weehawken, New Jersey, in July 1804.

12
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.880
[George] Hamilton's death made Burr a political pariah in the East.

13
00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:10.200
[George] Realizing his national career was shattered, Burr turned his restless gaze to the vast, untamed Western frontier, where Spanish territory was weak and American settlers were hungry for conquest and new empires.

14
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:17.320
[George] Burr was a master of political organization who had unified the working class voters of New York through the Society of St.

15
00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:22.120
[George] Tammany, creating the prototype for the modern American political machine.

16
00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:34.680
[George] Burr's brilliant legal career in New York had established him as one of the most formidable courtroom strategists of his era, admired by peers for his razor-sharp cross-examinations and incisive legal briefs.

17
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:44.840
[George] Beginning in 1805, Burr embarked on a grand tour of the Western frontier, floating down the Ohio and Mississippi Rivers in a lavish 60-foot keelboat.

18
00:02:44.840 --> 00:03:00.120
[George] Burr courted wealthy frontier landowners, politicians and generals, none more crucial than Harmon Blender Hassett, an eccentric, wealthy Irish aristocrat who owned a private island in the Ohio River near modern-day Parkersburg, West Virginia.

19
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.680
[George] Blender Hassett Island became Burr's Western operational headquarters.

20
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:11.320
[George] Boatbuilders constructed flatboats, stored rifles, and drilled dozens of adventurous young recruits.

21
00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:28.360
[George] Burr's chief co-conspirator was General James Wilkinson, the commanding general of the United States Army and military governor of Upper Louisiana, who was also, unknown to American officials, secret Agent 13 in the paid espionage service of the Spanish Crown.

22
00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:32.360
[George] To this day, historians debate Burr's true objective.

23
00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:44.520
[George] Was he planning to liberate Spanish Mexico in the event of an anticipated war between Spain and the United States, or was he scheming to sever the Western states from the Union and crown himself emperor in New Orleans?

24
00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:56.400
[George] In 1806, Burr sent a fateful letter in complex numerical cipher to General Wilkinson, stating that the time has arrived to mobilize 500 armed men and march south.

25
00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:08.720
[George] Blener Hassett was so enthralled by Burr's vision of an empire in the West that he mortgaged his vast estate and island paradise, purchasing supplies and boats that were later seized by federal marshals.

26
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:21.680
[George] Frontier settlers in the Ohio Valley were captivated by Burr charisma and grand promises of Western expansion, eager to escape Eastern economic domination and build a thriving empire beyond the Mississippi.

27
00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:35.520
[George] When General Wilkinson received Burr's cipher letter at his military post in Natchetaches, Louisiana, the treacherous general realized that war with Spain was not imminent, and that Burr's small expedition was doomed to failure.

28
00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:44.240
[George] Calculating that saving the Union would earn him federal glory and protect his secret Spanish pension, Wilkinson ruthlessly betrayed Burr.

29
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:57.200
[George] Wilkinson altered the cipher letter to make Burr appear as a bloodthirsty traitor plotting to capture New Orleans and assassinate American officials, sending frantic warnings directly to President Thomas Jefferson in Washington.

30
00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:02.080
[George] Jefferson, long suspicious of Burr's motives, reacted with fury.

31
00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:14.800
[George] On November 27, 1806, Jefferson issued a sweeping presidential proclamation declaring that an unlawful military expedition had formed in the West and ordering the arrest of all conspirators.

32
00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:27.440
[George] Federal marshals and state militias raided Blener Hassett Island, burning the mansion, while Burr's small flotilla of sixty young men was surrounded by territorial authorities near Natchez, Mississippi.

33
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:44.360
[George] Realizing Wilkinson had betrayed him, Burr fled into the wilderness disguised in ragged riverboat clothes, but was recognized and captured in Alabama in February 1807, escorted under heavy military guard to Richmond, Virginia, to face trial for high treason.

34
00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:56.280
[George] Wilkinson's betrayal was motivated by pure self-preservation, as the corrupt general realized that his covert Spanish payments would be discovered if Burr expedition provoked a full-scale war.

35
00:05:56.280 --> 00:06:08.040
[George] President Jefferson received daily updates from federal marshals tracking Burr movements, determined to crush what he viewed as an existential threat to the unity and stability of the young American republic.

36
00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:18.840
[George] The trial of Aaron Burr opened on August 3, 1807, in the Virginia State Capitol in Richmond, transforming the city into a buzzing national amphitheater.

37
00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:32.120
[George] Hundreds of spectators, journalists, politicians, and curiosity seekers crowded into the courtroom in the stifling summer heat, including young Washington Irving, who sent colorful dispatches to New York newspapers.

38
00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:38.760
[George] The trial pitted the two greatest legal minds and ideological titans of the early republic against one another.

39
00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:50.600
[George] Presiding over the United States Circuit Court was Chief Justice John Marshall, a staunch federalist who believed in a strong independent judiciary and constitutional checks on presidential power.

40
00:06:50.600 --> 00:07:07.160
[George] Directing the prosecution from the White House was President Thomas Jefferson, who was so obsessed with hanging Burr that he personally directed the legal strategy, drafted subpoena lists, and offered federal pardons to any witness willing to testify against his former vice president.

41
00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:12.920
[George] Publicly declaring Burr's guilt, placed beyond all question before the trial even began.

42
00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:23.680
[George] Spectators traveled from across the nation to witness the courtroom duel, packing the galleries in sweltering August heat as Burr and his defense team clashed with the federal prosecutors.

43
00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:34.800
[George] The trial in Richmond was a grand legal spectacle featuring the finest legal orators of the age, drawing crowds that spilled out of the courthouse onto the Capitol grounds under the summer sun.

44
00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:46.640
[George] Early in the proceedings, Burr and his brilliant defense team, led by eccentric Maryland legal genius Luther Martin, known as the Federal Bulldog, dropped a constitutional bombshell.

45
00:07:46.640 --> 00:08:01.120
[George] Burr requested that Chief Justice Marshall issue a subpoena duches Tecum ordering President Thomas Jefferson to appear in court and turn over confidential presidential correspondence, including General Wilkinson's original unedited letters.

46
00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:13.200
[George] Jefferson was incandescent with rage, arguing that the President of the United States was immune from judicial summons and that yielding to Marshall would make the executive branch subordinate to the judiciary.

47
00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:22.480
[George] In a landmark ruling that established the foundation of modern American constitutional law, Marshall ruled emphatically that the president was not a monarch.

48
00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:25.920
[George] The president of the United States is subject to the law.

49
00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:32.880
[George] In the provisions of the Constitution and the laws of the United States, there is no exception extending to the president.

50
00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:47.840
[George] While Jefferson refused to appear in person, citing executive duties, he complied by submitting the requested documents to the court, establishing the principle that even the American president is subject to the subpoenas of the federal judiciary.

51
00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:59.920
[George] Marshall's decision to subpoena the president established the critical precedent of executive accountability, demonstrating that the American judiciary had the authority to compel the executive branch to provide evidence.

52
00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:12.200
[George] John Marshall's courageous defense of judicial independence established that no executive official, not even the President of the United States, stands above the binding authority of the federal courts.

53
00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:24.760
[George] The prosecution, led by brilliant Virginia federal attorney George Hay and renowned orator William Wirt, called dozens of witnesses to paint Burr as a traitorous mastermind who had planned to overthrow the government.

54
00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:41.720
[George] But Marshall insisted on a strict, narrow reading of Article III, Section 3 of the United States Constitution, which states, treason against the United States shall consist only in levying war against them, or in adhering to their enemies, giving them aid and comfort.

55
00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:50.440
[George] No person shall be convicted of treason unless on the testimony of two witnesses to the same overt act, or on confession in open court.

56
00:09:50.440 --> 00:10:03.480
[George] Marshall noted that the Founding Fathers had deliberately restricted treason to prevent ruling administrations from using treason charges as a political weapon to execute their political rivals, as British monarchs had done for centuries.

57
00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:11.640
[George] Under Marshall's rigorous legal doctrine, conspiring to commit treason was not treason, talking about an empire was not treason.

58
00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:19.400
[George] The prosecution had to prove an actual overt act of war, witnessed by two people in the jurisdiction where the trial took place.

59
00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:32.760
[George] Because Burr was hundreds of miles away in Kentucky when recruits assembled on Blener Hassett Island, and because no shots had ever been fired, Marshall ruled that all circumstantial evidence and rumors of conspiracy were inadmissible.

60
00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:45.840
[George] Marshall's narrow constitutional definition of treason was one of the most important judicial opinions in American history, protecting freedom of political speech and preventing treason trials from becoming tools of tyranny.

61
00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:58.720
[George] The strict constitutional requirement of two witnesses to an overt act of war established an unshakable barrier against politically motivated treason trials, safeguarding American democracy for centuries.

62
00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:04.960
[George] Stripped of their conspiracy evidence by Marshall's ruling, the prosecution's case collapsed completely.

63
00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:12.800
[George] On September 1, 1807, after 25 minutes of deliberation, the jury returned with a verdict of not guilty.

64
00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:23.600
[George] President Jefferson was furious, drafting plans to impeach John Marshall, but Marshall's courageous, principled ruling stood as an eternal monument to the rule of law.

65
00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:37.360
[George] By establishing that treason could never be used as a political weapon against government critics, and by confirming that the president was accountable to the federal courts, John Marshall preserved the constitutional liberties of future generations.

66
00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:42.160
[George] Though legally acquitted, Aaron Burr was ruined in the court of public opinion.

67
00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:52.240
[George] He spent four years in self-imposed exile wandering Europe in poverty before returning to New York to practice law in quiet obscurity until his death in 1836.

68
00:11:52.240 --> 00:12:08.400
[George] Today, the Burr Treason trial stands as a monumental triumph of American constitutional jurisprudence, proving that the true strength of a democracy lies not in the power to crush its enemies, but in the courage to guarantee the rule of law to its most despised defendants.

69
00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:20.800
[George] The Burr trial confirmed the brilliance of the American constitutional design, proving that an independent judiciary could withstand executive pressure and protect the foundational rights of individual citizens.

70
00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:32.840
[George] The acquittal of Aaron Burr affirmed that the United States is a nation governed by constitutional law and objective evidence, not the political passions or executive decrees of the governing majority.

71
00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:40.840
[Rebecca] The Burr Treason trial preserved the constitutional limits of executive power and defined the meaning of American liberty.

72
00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:42.200
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

73
00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:47.960
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey into the trackless wilderness of the great dismal swamp.

